# =========================================================================
# server.bcyr — benchmarks for the HTTP request path.
#
# No oracle bench file: `rust-old/benches/` has no server harness, because the
# Rust request path is axum's and benchmarking it would have measured tokio.
# The port's path is `agnosai_route_dispatch`, which is ordinary synchronous
# code, so it can be timed directly.
#
# ## What these exist to prove
#
# B2 threads the per-request arena through the read routes, and
# `tests/server_serve.tcyr` proves the **memory** claim — dispatch through
# `dispatch_a` charges the global bump nothing beyond the router's match
# struct. That measurement says nothing about **time**, and threading is not
# free by construction: every `_a` call carries an extra argument, and
# `alloc_via` is one indirection more than `alloc`.
#
# So each threaded route is timed on both arms. The paired rows are the claim:
# threading buys the memory without costing latency. A regression in either
# direction shows up as one row moving against the other.
#
# ## Why the global arm is the slower one anyway
#
# The global allocator is a no-free bump: a request's garbage is never
# reclaimed, so a long-lived process walks an ever-growing heap and loses cache
# locality that the arena — reset to its base between requests — keeps. The
# arena arm pays one `reset_via` per iteration, which is a pointer store.
#
# `route_resolve` is timed on its own because it is the residual: the match
# struct is minted before dispatch reaches any arm, so it is in **both** numbers
# and neither route threading nor any further handler work can remove it.
#
# These are CYRIUS baseline numbers, NOT comparable to
# rust-old/bench-history.csv. See CLAUDE.md.
#
# Stdlib is auto-prepended from cyrius.cyml — do NOT include stdlib modules.
# =========================================================================

